---
title: SvelteKit
name: SvelteKit
description: Wire a contact form to Postboi with a one-line SvelteKit action.
category: Frameworks
---

`postboi/kit` reads `FormData`, sends it, and returns `{ success: true }`, or
`fail(400, { error })` on failure. A contact-form action is a single line.

```typescript
// +page.server.ts
import { mail } from 'postboi/kit'

export const actions = { default: mail }
```

## The form

Point a `multipart/form-data` form at the action. Field names use the
[`fieldset→field`](/formdata#grouped-fields) syntax to group related fields, and
`_subject` (and friends) set the email's [special fields](/formdata#special-fields).

Wire up a hidden `_reply_to` field bound to the sender's email. Then, when the
notification lands in your inbox, hitting **Reply** goes straight back to the person who
filled in the form, not to your no-reply `from` address.

For most contact forms, a reply-to address is all you need: keep `from` as your default
sending address and let reply-to carry the conversation. Only set a per-message `from`
when you want the email itself to appear from a specific address.

```svelte
<!-- +page.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
	import { enhance } from '$app/forms'

	let email = $state('')
</script>

<form method="POST" use:enhance enctype="multipart/form-data">
	<input type="hidden" name="_subject" value="Contact Form" />
	<input type="hidden" name="_reply_to" value={email} />
	<input
		type="text"
		name="_honey"
		tabindex="-1"
		autocomplete="off"
		aria-hidden="true"
		style="position: absolute; left: -9999px; height: 0; width: 0; opacity: 0"
	/>
	<input name="contact→name" placeholder="Name" required />
	<input name="contact→email" type="email" placeholder="Email" required bind:value={email} />
	<textarea name="details→message" placeholder="Message"></textarea>
	<input type="file" name="details→attachments" multiple />
	<button type="submit">Send</button>
</form>
```

The submitted `FormData` becomes a tidy HTML table in the email body. See
[FormData](/formdata) for how the table is built.

The hidden `_honey` field is the built-in honeypot: bots fill it, humans can't see it, and a
filled honeypot skips the send while the action still returns `{ success: true }`: the
bot learns nothing. Want more? [Spam protection](/spam) covers the invisible captcha too:
fully managed on the Postboi provider (one script tag, no keys), or bring your own Turnstile with
a single env var.

## Or use the Captcha component

`postboi/svelte` ships that honeypot (plus the [managed invisible captcha](/spam) on
the Postboi provider) as a single prop-free component. Your form stays a native `<form>`
(`use:enhance` and form libraries keep working); just drop it inside:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
	import { enhance } from '$app/forms'
	import Captcha from 'postboi/svelte'
</script>

<form method="POST" use:enhance enctype="multipart/form-data">
	<!-- your fields… -->
	<Captcha />
	<button type="submit">Send</button>
</form>
```

The publishable key is baked in by `bunx postboi sync`. See
[Spam protection](/spam#the-captcha-component).

A full, runnable version of this form lives in
[`examples/sveltekit-provider-postboi`](https://github.com/postboi-mail/postboi/tree/main/examples/sveltekit-provider-postboi).

## Remote functions

SvelteKit's [remote functions](https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/remote-functions) (experimental)
let a library ship the whole backend. `postboi/remote` exports a ready-made remote form:
no `+page.server.ts`, no action, no endpoint. The component *is* the app:

```svelte
<!-- +page.svelte -->
<script lang="ts">
	import { mail } from 'postboi/remote'
	import Captcha from 'postboi/svelte'

	let email = $state('')
</script>

<form {...mail} enctype="multipart/form-data">
	<input {...mail.fields._subject.as('hidden', 'Contact Form')} />
	<input {...mail.fields._reply_to.as('hidden', email)} />
	<Captcha />

	<input {...mail.fields.contact.name.as('text')} placeholder="Name" required />
	<input {...mail.fields.contact.email.as('email')} placeholder="Email" required bind:value={email} />
	<textarea {...mail.fields.details.message.as('text')} placeholder="Message"></textarea>
	<input {...mail.fields.details.attachments.as('file')} multiple />

	<button disabled={!!mail.pending}>{mail.pending ? 'Sending…' : 'Send'}</button>
</form>

{#if mail.result?.success}
	<p>Thanks, we'll be in touch!</p>
{:else if mail.result}
	<p>{mail.result.error}</p>
{/if}
```

Two lines of setup, since the feature is experimental:

```js
// svelte.config.js — or inline in the sveltekit() vite plugin
kit: { experimental: { remoteFunctions: true } }
```

```ts
// vite.config.ts — remote modules must reach the SvelteKit transform,
// not Vite's dependency prebundle. `bunx postboi init` adds this for you.
optimizeDeps: { exclude: ['postboi/remote'] }
```

> The [`postboi/vite`](/config#bundled-and-deployed-servers) plugin sets that exclude itself, so if you're
> already using it (for edge deploys, say) you can drop the line.

Worth knowing:

- **Field names nest instead of using arrows.** Remote-form names must be valid JS paths,
  so `fields.contact.name` replaces `contact→name`, and the email's
  [grouped table](/formdata#grouped-fields) comes out identical. Special fields
  (`_subject`, `_reply_to`, …) work unchanged.
- **No schema required.** A contact form is arbitrary fields by design, so the handler
  accepts whatever the form sends: spam checks and parsing happen in the send pipeline,
  same as the classic action. Want client-side validation? Compose kit's own preflight:
  `<form {...mail.preflight(schema)}>` with any Standard Schema (Zod, Valibot).
- **Enhancement is built in.** The spread progressively enhances the form (no
  `use:enhance` import) and resets it after a successful submit. `mail.pending` counts
  in-flight submissions, `mail.result` is the (ephemeral) return value. Custom submit
  behaviour uses the form's own method: `<form {...mail.enhance(async (form) => { await
  form.submit(); … })}>`. Without JavaScript the form still posts and renders the result,
  exactly like a native action.
- **Spam protection carries over.** `<Captcha />` renders the honeypot under a
  remote-safe name, and the managed captcha detects remote forms automatically. A tripped
  honeypot still reports `{ success: true }` to the bot.
- **A failed send doesn't throw.** `mail.result` becomes `{ success: false, error }`, with
  the reason in `error`, so handle it in the markup (the `{:else if mail.result}` branch
  above) rather than in a `try` around the submit.
- **Checkboxes read like answers.** A ticked box (`true`) is "Yes" in the email, and an
  unticked one (`false`) is left out rather than listed as "false".

### A remote form with a schema

`remote()` uses SvelteKit's `unchecked` mode, so it accepts whatever the form submits and
the spam fields arrive intact. For typed fields and `issues()`, pass a
[Standard Schema](https://standardschema.dev) (valibot, zod, arktype) first. Write it for
your own fields only: `remote()` keeps `_honey` and `_captcha` out of your schema and puts
them back after, so SvelteKit's validation can't drop them.

```ts
// contact.remote.ts
import * as v from 'valibot'
import { remote } from 'postboi/kit'

const schema = v.object({
	name: v.pipe(v.string(), v.minLength(1)),
	email: v.pipe(v.string(), v.email())
})

export const contact = remote(schema, ({ value }) => ({
	reply_to: value.email,
	subject: `New enquiry from ${value.name}`,
	// Runs once the email has gone; what it returns is merged into the result.
	after: async () => ({ subscribed: await subscribe(value.email) })
}))
```

```svelte
<form {...contact}>
	<Captcha />
	<input {...contact.fields.name.as('text')} />
	{#each contact.fields.name.issues() as issue}<p>{issue.message}</p>{/each}
	<input {...contact.fields.email.as('email')} />
	<button>Send</button>
</form>
{#if contact.result?.success}
	<p>Thanks{contact.result.subscribed ? ', and welcome aboard' : ''}!</p>
{:else if contact.result}
	<p>{contact.result.error}</p>
{/if}
```

`value` is the post as your schema returned it. The honeypot, the captcha token, the
visitor's IP and a failed send are handled as they are for any `remote()`. A tripped
honeypot returns `{ success: true }` without running `after`, and an `after` that throws is
your error rather than a failed send, so it isn't caught.

With a Postboi captcha key baked in, a form that arrives with no token at all is said once
in the server's log. That's the sign a form library or a schema of your own dropped it.

`_captcha` rather than `cf-turnstile-response`: remote-form field names must be valid JS
paths, so the managed loader renames the token field. postboi strips both before rendering.

> Sending through a **bring-your-own provider** while `<Captcha />` is on the page fails
> with `captcha_misconfigured`: a token arrived with no secret to verify it against.
> Managed captcha needs the Postboi provider; on any other one, configure Turnstile
> yourself or opt out with `captcha: { turnstile: false }`. The **mock** provider is the
> exception: it's credential-free by design, so it drops the token and sends anyway.

For a custom provider or forced fields, build your own in a `.remote.ts` file with
`remote()` from `postboi/kit`, the remote counterpart of `action()`:

```ts
// src/lib/mail.remote.ts
import { remote } from 'postboi/kit'
import Resend from 'postboi/resend'
import { RESEND_API_KEY } from '$env/static/private'

const resend = new Resend({ api_key: RESEND_API_KEY, default: { from: 'no-reply@example.com' } })

export const contact = remote(resend, { to: 'team@example.com' })
```

## Testing actions

`postboi/kit` imports under `bun test` or plain Node with no `$app/server` mock. Outside
Vite it resolves to a build that leaves `$app/server` alone, so `action()`, `mail` and
`webhook()` work as they do in the app. `remote()` is the one export that needs SvelteKit,
and says so if you call it there.

```ts
import { action } from 'postboi/kit'
import Mock from 'postboi/mock'

const mail = new Mock({ default: { to: 'team@example.com', from: 'site@example.com' } })
const body = new FormData()
body.set('name', 'Ada')
await action(mail)({ request: new Request('http://x', { method: 'POST', body }) } as never)
expect(mail.last?.html).toContain('Ada')
```

## Options from the post

When the options depend on the submission, the request or a CMS, pass a function instead of
an object. It gets the request `event` and the post as `data`, parsed once, and returns the
send options:

```typescript
import { action } from 'postboi/kit'

export const actions = {
	default: action(async ({ event, field }) => ({
		to: await lookup(field('_blok')),
		subject: `New enquiry from ${field('name') ?? 'the website'}`,
		reply_to: field('email'),
		form: event.locals.site.form_name,
	}))
}
```

- `field(name)` reads one text field, trimmed, and gives `undefined` when it's missing or
  blank. Reach for it over `String(data.get(name))`, which turns a missing field into the
  string `"null"`: a reply-to of "null", or a CMS lookup for `stories/null`.
  `field.all(name)` does the same for a field posted more than once (a checkbox group, a
  list of uploaded file URLs) and gives every non-blank value, or `[]`. Both trim, so a
  check that counts characters exactly, like a `maxlength` where a line break is one
  character, reads `data` itself.
- A reply-to that isn't an address (a visitor typing `asdf` in the email box) is dropped
  with a warning rather than failing the send, so a typo doesn't cost you the submission.
- `data` is the post. Change it if you need to: validate it, delete fields, add some.
- Return `fail(…)` from `@sveltejs/kit` to stop the send. `action()` hands it back as is.
- A `_` field you read that isn't one of postboi's own (`_subject`, `_reply_to`, `_form`
  and the rest) never reaches the email, so an id can travel in a hidden input instead of
  the action's URL. A `_` field you don't read renders as before.
- The honeypot runs first. A bot that fills it gets `{ success: true }` and your function
  never runs, so nothing is looked up or fetched on its behalf.
- The visitor's IP, `is_spam` and `fail` on a send error all work as they do with an
  object.
- A blank string counts as unset, so an empty CMS field falls through to the post or
  `default.*`. `from` takes any address here (the API still refuses one your account can't
  send from, with `from_not_allowed`).

`remote()` takes the same function. There a returned `fail(…)` becomes
`{ success: false, error }`, using `error` from its data when there is one.

### Recipe: form recipients from a CMS

Each form block keeps its own recipient, sender and subject in the CMS. The form posts
which block it is, and the server looks it up, so a visitor can't pick the recipient:

```svelte
<form method="POST" action="/mail">
	<input type="hidden" name="_story" value={story.uuid} />
	<input type="hidden" name="_blok" value={block._uid} />
	<!-- the form's own fields -->
</form>
```

```typescript
// src/routes/mail/+page.server.ts
import { fail } from '@sveltejs/kit'
import { action, type FieldReader } from 'postboi/kit'

export const actions = {
	default: action(async ({ field }) => {
		const block = await find_form(field)
		if (!block) return fail(404, { error: 'Form not found' })
		// Blank fields in the CMS fall through to default.to, default.from and the post.
		return { to: block.to, from: block.from, subject: block.subject, form: block.title || true }
	})
}

// `FieldReader` is the type of `field`, for helpers that read the post themselves.
async function find_form(field: FieldReader) {
	const story = field('_story')
	const uid = field('_blok')
	if (!story || !uid) return undefined
	const { data } = await storyblok.get(`cdn/stories/${story}`, { find_by: 'uuid' })
	return find_block(data.story.content, uid)
}
```

`_story` and `_blok` are read, so neither shows up in the email. `form: true` files an
untitled block as a form submission without letting a posted `_form` choose one.

### Recipe: reply-to and subject from the fields

No hidden inputs mirroring the email field, and no subject built in the browser:

```typescript
export const actions = {
	default: action(({ field }) => ({
		reply_to: field('email'),
		subject: `${field('topic') ?? 'General'} enquiry from ${field('name') ?? 'the website'}`
	}))
}
```

postboi doesn't guess the reply-to from a field called `email` for you: plenty of forms
have an email field that isn't the sender's, and a guessed reply-to would go to the wrong
person without anyone noticing.

### Recipe: attachments uploaded first

When the browser uploads files first and posts their URLs, fetch them into `File`s and drop
the field. Only fetch from the host you uploaded to, or the post decides what your server
requests:

```typescript
export const actions = {
	default: action(async ({ data }) => {
		const urls = data.getAll('Images').map(String)
		data.delete('Images')
		const files = await Promise.all(
			urls
				.filter((url) => new URL(url).origin === UPLOADS_ORIGIN)
				.map(async (url) => {
					const response = await fetch(url)
					return new File([await response.blob()], new URL(url).pathname.split('/').pop()!)
				})
		)
		return { attachments: files }
	})
}
```

## Using a configured instance

Got a configured provider instance? Wrap it with `action()`. Any send option you pass
is merged into every send, and `status` sets the failure code.

```typescript
import Resend from 'postboi/resend'
import { action } from 'postboi/kit'
import { RESEND_API_KEY, EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS } from '$env/static/private'

const mail = new Resend({ api_key: RESEND_API_KEY, default: { from: EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS } })

export const actions = {
	default: action(mail, { status: 422, to: 'team@example.com' })
}
```
